1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:09,206
Price broke the previous day's low. Everyone's stop loss got hit.

2
00:00:09,655 --> 00:00:15,690
And then? Price went straight up. As if someone was waiting for exactly those stops.

3
00:00:16,140 --> 00:00:20,502
Today we find out: coincidence, or a plan.

4
00:00:21,502 --> 00:00:26,412
Hello, this is episode seventeen of the SPIDER Order Flow series.

5
00:00:26,863 --> 00:00:33,133
Last time: absorption and exhaustion. Force came in, but price did not move.

6
00:00:33,583 --> 00:00:40,113
Today: liquidity pools and stop hunts. This is where the footprints of big players begin.

7
00:00:41,113 --> 00:00:46,599
Where do most buyers place their stop loss? Just below a clean low.

8
00:00:47,049 --> 00:00:53,658
In our example: 2640. The previous day's low, touched twice.

9
00:00:54,108 --> 00:01:00,142
Below it sit about 1,500 contracts of sell stops. That is a liquidity pool.

10
00:01:01,142 --> 00:01:06,942
Remember episode three: a stop order turns into a market order once triggered.

11
00:01:07,392 --> 00:01:13,033
So the moment 2640 breaks, 1,500 contracts come in to sell at once.

12
00:01:14,033 --> 00:01:18,396
Now picture a big buyer. He needs to buy 1,300 contracts.

13
00:01:18,847 --> 00:01:26,473
On a normal day there are only about 200 sellers per price. Buy all at once, and price runs away from him.

14
00:01:26,923 --> 00:01:32,880
He needs lots of sellers in one place. And where are they? In the stops.

15
00:01:33,880 --> 00:01:37,589
Price dipped to 2636. The stops fired.

16
00:01:38,039 --> 00:01:48,149
Look at the footprint: the bottom four rows have bid volume 380, 520, 440, 160. Total 1,500.

17
00:01:48,598 --> 00:01:58,786
And ask on those same rows: 300, 480, 410, 120. Total 1,310. Someone was buying it all.

18
00:01:59,786 --> 00:02:07,023
The candle low is 2636, but it closes at 2643. A long lower wick.

19
00:02:07,473 --> 00:02:13,429
Delta just minus 190. Heavy selling, yet price is back above the level.

20
00:02:13,879 --> 00:02:17,039
After that, price went up to 2655.

21
00:02:18,039 --> 00:02:22,715
Now another day. The same level, 2640, breaks.

22
00:02:23,165 --> 00:02:30,558
But this time the candle closes at 2634. The next at 2628, then 2625.

23
00:02:31,007 --> 00:02:38,714
Delta minus 420, then minus 380. No big buyer showed up. The level was never reclaimed.

24
00:02:39,714 --> 00:02:47,420
Sweep: the level breaks, but the candle closes back above. Heavy volume below, but weak delta.

25
00:02:47,870 --> 00:02:54,870
Breakout: closes below the level, the next candles stay below, delta keeps negative.

26
00:02:55,321 --> 00:02:59,527
The clearest sign: did price reclaim the level?

27
00:03:00,527 --> 00:03:06,718
Do not jump in on a guess. Let a candle close back above 2640.

28
00:03:07,168 --> 00:03:14,716
Stop below the sweep low, like 2634. Target: the previous high or POC.

29
00:03:15,167 --> 00:03:21,593
And remember: do not put your own stop right under a clean low. Everyone puts it there.

30
00:03:22,593 --> 00:03:29,124
Spot forex and gold do not give real bid and ask volume. Footprints come from futures.

31
00:03:29,573 --> 00:03:35,843
But where stops pile up shows on any price chart: clean highs and lows.

32
00:03:36,843 --> 00:03:43,844
So, today in short. Stops pile up behind clean highs and lows: a liquidity pool.

33
00:03:44,293 --> 00:03:52,000
Big players need that liquidity to fill. A sweep means: stops taken, and price comes back.

34
00:03:52,450 --> 00:03:56,891
Breakout versus sweep: was the level reclaimed or not.

35
00:03:57,891 --> 00:04:04,734
Next episode: iceberg orders and spoofing. Hidden and fake orders in the book.

36
